{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786538915,"nonce":"gqESeV7PSebOvIP5v9c44/0nt3c=","title":"**What is wrong.** `af81349` wired `internal/platform/idempotency` into the four operations whose response carries a secret — `createApiToken`, `revokeApiToken`, `rotateSourceIngestToken`, `createSource`. Twenty-eight operations declare `parameters/IdempotencyKeyHeader` in `api/openapi/openapi.yaml` (verified by counting every `$ref: '#/components/parameters/IdempotencyKeyHeader'` line: 1004, 1052, 1100, 1182, 1253, 1805, 1856, 1919, 1971, 2240, 2313, 2358, 2434, 2479, 2693, 2736, 2846, 2917, 2965, 3007, 3076, 3124, 3166, 3211, 3484, 4242, 4480, 4528 — 28 sites). Subtracting the four now guarded leaves **24** that still declare the header and read it nowhere: `ackAlert`, `unackAlert`, `commentOnAlert`, `snoozeAlert`, `unsnoozeAlert`, `ackAlertGroup`, `commentOnAlertGroup`, `snoozeAlertGroup`, `unsnoozeAlertGroup`, `updateSource`, `deleteSource`, `reconcileSource`, `createCluster`, `updateCluster`, `createChannel`, `updateChannel`, `deleteChannel`, `testChannel`, `createNotificationPolicy`, `previewNotificationPolicy`, `updateNotificationPolicy`, `deleteNotificationPolicy`, `retryDelivery`, `login`.","message":"The parameter's own description, `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:4972-4974`, still promises this for all of them: *\"Replaying the same key with the same body within the retention window returns the original result rather than acting twice; replaying it with a different body is a `409`.\"* The carve-out at `4988-4989` names exactly the four fixed operations — *\"`createSource`, `createApiToken`, `revokeApiToken` and `rotateSourceIngestToken` **refuse a replay rather than replaying it**\"* — so the promise for the other 24 is unqualified and, for several of them, false.\n\nThe frontend already mints a key per gesture — `web/src/lib/format.ts:185-186`, `idempotencyKey()` returning `globalThis.crypto.randomUUID()` — and threads it through `web/src/api/client.ts:194-201` (`idempotencyKey` on `RequestOptions`, sent as the `Idempotency-Key` header). `web/src/api/endpoints.ts` sends one on **14** operations: `ackAlert` (:271), `unackAlert` (:277), `commentOnAlert` (:282), `snoozeAlert` (:297), `unsnoozeAlert` (:307), `ackAlertGroup` (:382), `commentOnAlertGroup` (:389), `snoozeAlertGroup` (:405), `unsnoozeAlertGroup` (:415), `createCluster` (:448), `createSource` (:464, now honoured), `createChannel` (:585), `createPolicy` i.e. `createNotificationPolicy` (:606), `retryDelivery` (:637). One of the 14 is now safe; **13 are sent by a client that believes it is retrying safely and is not.**\n\n**The demonstration.** The 24 are not one failure mode; verified per group:\n\n*Ack / unack / unsnooze — SAFE, but not by a header.* `internal/alerts/domain/lifecycle.go:904-907` — `Acknowledge` on an already-acked occurrence returns `errs.Precondition(\"already_acked\", ...)`, not a second ack event. `lifecycle.go:954-957` — `Unacknowledge` on a not-acked occurrence returns `not_acked`. `internal/alerts/service/actions.go:399-401` — `Unsnooze` on a non-snoozed alert returns `not_snoozed`. A retry after success gets an error instead of the promised replay — a UX gap, not a duplicate.\n\n*Snooze / snoozeAlertGroup — UNSAFE.* `internal/alerts/service/actions.go:289-377`: `Snooze` never checks \"have I already granted this snooze.\" If a snooze is already active it ends it as `superseded` (:315-325, `endSnooze` at :440) and inserts a brand-new row with a plain `INSERT` — no `ON CONFLICT` (`internal/alerts/repository/snooze.go`). `notifySnoozeChange` fires again at `actions.go:366`. A retried snooze produces two rows (one `superseded`, one live) and a second outbound \"snoozed\" notification for one user click. `snoozeAlertGroup` is not a separate implementation: `internal/grouping/service/read.go:327-334`'s `Snooze` fans out and calls `s.actions.SnoozeAs` per currently-joined member, and `SnoozeAs` (`internal/alerts/service/seam.go:138-148`) calls straight into the same `Service.Snooze` — so a retried group snooze repeats the same supersede-and-recreate, and the same duplicate notification, once per member.\n\n*Comment / commentOnAlertGroup — UNSAFE, and the \"ON CONFLICT\" in this file does not cover it.* `internal/alerts/repository/event.go:117-121` has a real dedupe mechanism — `INSERT INTO alert_event_keys (...) ON CONFLICT (org_id, dedupe_key) DO NOTHING`. But `Comment`'s key, `actions.go:227`, is `\"comment:\" + alertID + \":\" + now.Format(time.RFC3339Nano)`, where `now := s.Now()` (`actions.go:210`) is the wall clock read fresh inside *this* call. A genuine retry — a second HTTP request — computes a different nanosecond timestamp, hence a different key, so the `ON CONFLICT` never fires between an original and its retry. A retried comment appends a second, distinct row to the timeline. `commentOnAlertGroup` fans out onto the identical `Comment` call (`internal/grouping/service/read.go:293-319` → `CommentAs` → `seam.go:170-179` → `s.Comment`), so the group form duplicates once per member too.\n\n*createCluster / createChannel / createNotificationPolicy — safe by accident, the same shape `createSource` had before this fix.* Plain `INSERT`, no `ON CONFLICT`, but each carries a `UNIQUE(org_id, ...)` on a client-supplied field: `clusters_key_uniq` (`db/migrations/00004_sources.sql:23`), `channels_name_uniq` (`db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:68`), `policies_name_uniq` (`00011_notification.sql:118`). A same-body retry gets a `409` on the name/key rather than a silent duplicate row — but it is a hard conflict, not the replay the contract promises, and the client is never told the id of what it already created.\n\n*testChannel — UNSAFE, and the UI doesn't even try.* `internal/channels/api/channels.go:329-352` calls `rt.tester.Test(ctx, scope, id)` unconditionally on every request — no dedup of any kind. Two calls send two real outbound test messages to Slack or the webhook. Separately, `web/src/api/endpoints.ts:597-599`'s `testChannel()` sends no `idempotencyKey` at all, so this is doubly unfixed: nothing reads the header, and nothing sends one.\n\n*retryDelivery — SAFE, and by design.* `internal/notification/repository/config.go:592-597`: `requeueDeliverySQL` is one atomic `UPDATE notification_deliveries SET status='pending', ... WHERE org_id=$1 AND id=$2 AND status='dead'`, with the predicate placed inside the `UPDATE` rather than a prior `SELECT` specifically to avoid a race (comment at :599-605: *\"a read-then-write would race a worker that is claiming the same row... nudging one would double-send\"*). `internal/notification/api/audit.go:301-317`: a second call finds `requeued=false` and answers `412 delivery_not_dead` instead of re-enqueuing.\n\n*login* declares the header (contract) but the client deliberately never sends a key — `web/src/api/endpoints.ts:671-673`'s comment: *\"No idempotency key: the contract accepts one, but a retried login is not a duplicated side effect the way a retried acknowledgement is, and minting a key per keystroke-driven submit would key the rate limiter's own evidence.\"* — a defensible design choice, unlike the other 23.\n\n**Why it matters.** Only three of the 24 — `snoozeAlert`/`snoozeAlertGroup`, `commentOnAlert`/`commentOnAlertGroup`, and `testChannel` — genuinely duplicate a side effect on retry; the ack/unack/unsnooze family and `retryDelivery` fail closed on their own state machines, and the three creates fail closed on a DB unique constraint. But the contract's `IdempotencyKeyHeader` description makes an unqualified promise to all 24, and the UI is sending client-minted keys on 13 of them in the belief that a dropped response is safe to retry. A dropped response to a snooze or a comment — during exactly the network conditions the header exists for — produces a second Slack notification and a duplicate timeline entry, not the replay the contract advertises. `testChannel` is the sharpest case: every retry, deliberate or not, is a second real message sent to a customer's own Slack workspace or webhook.\n\n**Where.** `api/openapi/openapi.yaml` (`components/parameters/IdempotencyKeyHeader` at :4967-4999, the 28 `$ref` sites listed above); `internal/alerts/domain/lifecycle.go:891-942` (ack/unack guards); `internal/alerts/service/actions.go:184-259` (`Comment`), `:289-377` (`Snooze`), `:382-433` (`Unsnooze`); `internal/alerts/repository/event.go:117-121` (the dedupe `ON CONFLICT` `Comment` defeats); `internal/channels/api/channels.go:329-352` (`testChannel`); `internal/notification/repository/config.go:592-605` and `internal/notification/api/audit.go:282-317` (`retryDelivery`, already safe); `db/migrations/00004_sources.sql:23`, `00011_notification.sql:68,118` (the accidental unique constraints); `internal/platform/idempotency/claim.go:221-225,285-293` (`Claim.CreatedRef` / `Result.Existing` — the field a correct fix for the three creates would use to replay the original resource instead of just refusing); `web/src/lib/format.ts:181-187`; `web/src/api/client.ts:190-201`; `web/src/api/endpoints.ts` (the 14 call sites named above).\n\n**Design constraint carried over from `af81349`.** `internal/platform/idempotency/doc.go:31-35`: *\"THE CLAIM PARTICIPATES IN THE CALLER'S TRANSACTION and refuses to run outside one.\"* Enforced, not just documented: `internal/platform/idempotency/repository.go:88-90` — `if !db.InTx(ctx) { return Result{}, errs.Internal(\"idempotency_claim_outside_tx\", errNoTx) }`. Every existing call site invokes `Claim` by hand inside its own handler's transaction; there is no generic response-replay middleware in oto, and there cannot be one that satisfies this invariant — a middleware that ran before a handler opened its transaction could never call `Claim` at all. Any fix here has to be wired per-handler, the same way the four secret-issuing endpoints were, not as a shared filter. `doc.go:4-13` further rules out caching the response itself as a general mechanism — irrelevant to the three creates and `testChannel` here, since none of their responses carry a secret, but the reason a general cache was rejected for the platform package still applies to any new shared helper someone reaches for.\n\n**Scope.** medium. Three distinct remedies, all buildable on the existing `internal/platform/idempotency` primitive: (1) `Comment` needs a key derived from the request, not the wall clock, so its existing `ON CONFLICT` actually catches a retry; (2) `Snooze`/`snoozeAlertGroup` needs either an ack-style precondition or a `Claim`-backed replay before it supersedes the incumbent; (3) `createCluster`, `createChannel`, `createNotificationPolicy` and `testChannel` need `Claim` wired in per-handler exactly as the four fixed operations were, using `CreatedRef` to replay the original resource for the three creates (they carry no secret, so replay — not refuse — is correct) and a claim-guarded skip for `testChannel`. `ackAlert`/`unackAlert`/`unsnoozeAlert`/`retryDelivery` need no data-safety fix, only a decision on whether their precondition errors should instead replay the original 200 to match the contract's literal wording.\n\n**Done when.** A retried `commentOnAlert`/`commentOnAlertGroup` with the same key and body replays the original event rather than appending a second; a retried `snoozeAlert`/`snoozeAlertGroup` with the same key and body replays the original snooze rather than superseding and recreating one; a retried `testChannel` with the same key and body replays the original test result rather than sending a second real notification; a retried `createCluster`/`createChannel`/`createNotificationPolicy` with the same key and body returns the original 201 (via `Claim.CreatedRef`) rather than a name-conflict `409`. `ackAlert`, `unackAlert`, `unsnoozeAlert` and `retryDelivery` are reviewed and either documented as an accepted exception to the contract's literal wording or brought into line with it. The contract's carve-out list at `openapi.yaml:4988` stays exactly the four operations that mint a secret.","files":null}]}